WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.102
Constraints turn important business rules into rules the database can enforce.

00:00:04.102 --> 00:00:06.762
NOT NULL says a value is required.

00:00:06.762 --> 00:00:09.547
DEFAULT supplies a value when one is omitted.

00:00:09.547 --> 00:00:13.452
UNIQUE protects candidate keys or other non-duplicate rules.

00:00:13.452 --> 00:00:13.842
CHECK evaluates a Boolean condition.

00:00:13.842 --> 00:00:16.107
PRIMARY KEY defines row identity.

00:00:16.107 --> 00:00:18.707
FOREIGN KEY protects relationships to parent rows.

00:00:18.707 --> 00:00:24.612
One beginner detail is especially important: CHECK does not automatically make a column required.

00:00:24.612 --> 00:00:30.962
For example, a check that quantity is greater than zero only evaluates the quantity when a value is present.

00:00:30.962 --> 00:00:34.047
If quantity must always be present, combine the check with NOT NULL.

00:00:34.047 --> 00:00:38.832
The database should enforce important rules whenever possible instead of relying only on application code or

00:00:38.832 --> 00:00:45.192
user memory. Connect this idea to a clinic system separating patients, encounters, clinicians, and billing.

00:00:45.192 --> 00:00:49.552
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:49.552 --> 00:00:55.967
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:55.967 --> 00:01:00.062
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:00.062 --> 00:01:04.798
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
